<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Restore configuration for a throwaway consumer that installs the package this repository
  just packed.

  That is the one check which proves the analyzer is not inert. Every test in the suite
  drives the analyzer classes directly, so none of them goes through analyzers/dotnet/cs —
  the only path NuGet reads to install a C# analyzer — and a pack that stopped producing it
  would install as a no-op with the whole suite green.

  Copy this file into a scratch directory OUTSIDE the repository and lay the rest out beside
  it. Every path below is relative to this file, so nothing here has to be edited:

      <scratch>/nuget.config     this file
      <scratch>/feed/            where the pack is written
      <scratch>/packages/        written by restore; delete it to start cold
      <scratch>/consumer/        dotnet new console, then dotnet add package Rendlio.Analyzers

  No sequence of two hyphens appears anywhere above, and none may: XML forbids it inside a
  comment, so a command line written out in full here stops NuGet parsing this file at all.

  globalPackagesFolder is the line the check hangs on. NuGet keys that folder by package id
  and version alone, and the version does not move between changes — so once this package
  has been extracted there it is never extracted again, whatever a fresh pack just wrote to
  the feed. Restore stays green and hands the compiler an assembly from some earlier build.
  The trap fires in both directions: a broken package reads green off a good cached copy,
  and a good package reads red off a stale one. A packages folder of its own is the only
  form of this check that cannot be fooled, and it is the reason a run starts by deleting
  the scratch directory rather than by remembering to clear anything machine-wide.

  fallbackPackageFolders is the same door under another name — a folder restore may satisfy
  an id and version out of without downloading. Visual Studio installs one machine-wide, so
  it is present on a developer's box whether or not anyone configured it.

  The two <clear /> elements drop what the machine contributes to each section. Sources,
  because a scratch feed has to be reachable at all. Mapping, because a user-level rule
  routing '*' at some other feed survives clearing the sources, and then answers NU1101 for
  a package sitting right there in the feed — an error that names the package rather than
  the rule that hid it. Rendlio.* is mapped at the feed rather than left to the '*' rule so
  that the package under test stays the one just packed once a version of the same name
  exists publicly: the longest matching pattern wins, so the mapping is what decides.
-->
<configuration>

  <config>
    <add key="globalPackagesFolder" value="packages" />
  </config>

  <fallbackPackageFolders>
    <clear />
  </fallbackPackageFolders>

  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="local" value="feed" />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <packageSourceMapping>
    <clear />
    <packageSource key="local">
      <package pattern="Rendlio.*" />
    </packageSource>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>

</configuration>
